\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Using the DC motor control example from the provided MathWorks link, perform the following:\
\pard\tx393\tx720\pardeftab720\li367\fi-368\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 a) Analyze the motor model parameters to determine the maximum continuous torque the motor can provide for the  given speed control scenario. Subsequently, calculate the Torque Constant (Kt) of the motor.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li373\fi-374\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 b) Obtain and plot the steady-state Speed-Torque Characteristic (mechanical output power curve) of the motor without any controller (i.e., operating directly from the supply voltage).\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li392\fi-393\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 c) Develop a position control system (output in radians) for the DC motor, using an appropriate control strategy (e.g., PID).\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li442\fi-443\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 d) Repeat the analyses and designs from parts a), b), and c) by modifying the model to represent a wound-field DC motor (if the original is permanent magnet, or vice-versa), ensuring you account for the field winding dynamics and excitation.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li423\fi-424\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 e) Illustrate the performance improvement of the control system (speed or position) when an anti-windup mechanism is implemented on the controller, especially under conditions involving actuator saturation (e.g., high command steps or current limits).\
	
